{"id": 82, "ts": 1780629681.0959628, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 4, "agent": "james_carter", "message": "I acknowledge Sarah\u2019s thorough recap of Candidate\u202fC\u2019s record\u2014100\u202f% reliability, calmness in a crisis, rapid correct decisions, solid technical grasp, and a positive crew atmosphere. Those are indeed strong safety\u2011critical attributes. However, the missing evidence on C\u2019s adaptability and feedback orientation remains a serious gap. His reluctance to pursue further education and the perception of egocentricity suggest a limited openness to new procedures or corrective input, which is essential for the evolving demands of long\u2011distance operations.\nBy contrast, Candidate\u202fB demonstrates concrete reliability through his \u201cvery conscientious\u201d demeanor, proven stress resilience (\u201chandles stress very well\u201d), and technical competence (accurate weather assessment and \u201cexcellent computer skills\u201d). While B\u2019s \u201cnasty remarks\u201d and weaker numeric memory are concerns, they are behavioral and attentional issues that can be mitigated with targeted coaching, clear communication protocols, and procedural safeguards. Moreover, B\u2019s conscientiousness directly supports operational reliability, and his stress handling meets our core requirement.\nGiven that Candidate\u202fC\u2019s adaptability risk is less remediable than B\u2019s behavioral concerns, I maintain my recommendation for Candidate\u202fB as the stronger overall fit for the long\u2011distance pilot role."}
